WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.440
Data protection begins with classification.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:17.060
AI systems may handle prompt data, uploaded files, retrieved documents, embeddings, vector store content, chat history, long-term memory, logs, transcripts, customer information, regulated data, and confidential business material.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.720
Each category needs rules for access, retention, redaction, encryption, and deletion.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:29.100
Treating all AI context as temporary text is a mistake because many systems store or reuse that context.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.040
Data minimization is a practical architecture control.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:37.140
The system should send only the data needed for the task, not every available field or document.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:43.760
Summaries, filtered snippets, structured outputs, or references may reduce exposure compared with full raw records.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:52.220
Redaction can remove secrets, credentials, personal data, or unnecessary internal details before content enters a model or log pipeline.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.920
Memory and retrieval require clear scope.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:02.580
Long-term memory can improve user experience, but it can also store sensitive, incorrect, stale, or unauthorized information.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.760
Retrieval systems can improve answer quality, but they can expose documents across tenants or preserve outdated content in indexes.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:19.340
Memory writes, memory reads, vector stores, and retrieval results need provenance, access control, retention rules, and deletion behavior.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.800
Internal knowledge should be separated from user-specific or tenant-specific data.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:31.620
A general policy document may be available broadly, while a customer record, private ticket, or uploaded file should follow stricter boundaries.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:38.240
Privacy review should be built into the design when customer data, regulated data, or employee information is involved.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.780
Secure architecture protects data before it becomes difficult to see where the data went.
